<BaseLayout toc>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <style is:inline>
    </style>
  </Fragment>

  <header>
    <TopicTags />
    <PageTitle />
    <PubDate />
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Preparation (after OS installation)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Install recommended NVIDIA drivers: Open the "Software & Updates"
        application, navigate to the "Additional Drivers" tab, and select the
        recommended NVIDIA driver for your GPU. Apply the changes and reboot
        when prompted. Alternatively, install via terminal:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo ubuntu-drivers devices
          sudo ubuntu-drivers autoinstall
          sudo reboot
        </ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        Power Management (Powertop): Linux's battery management on laptops
        tends to be less optimized than other operating systems. Powertop
        helps optimize power consumption and extend battery life. Install
        with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
          sudo apt install powertop tlp -y
          sudo systemctl enable tlp
        </ShellScript>
        Installing TLP removes <C>power-profiles-daemon</C>, so the power-mode
        selector in GNOME's quick settings goes away; TLP manages that instead.
      </li>

      <li>
        GNOME: I use GNOME desktop environment for simplicity. To further
        customize and optimize it, install GNOME Tweaks by running:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
          sudo apt install gnome-tweaks -y
        </ShellScript>
        You can use GNOME Tweaks to adjust various settings such as themes, fonts,
        and extensions to enhance your desktop experience. Here are some of my favorite
        GNOME extensions:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://extensions.gnome.org/extension/307/dash-to-dock/">Dash to Dock</a
            >: Turn the GNOME dash into a nice-looking dock for easier access
            to applications.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://extensions.gnome.org/extension/1460/caffeine-ng/">Caffeine-NG</a
            >: Prevents your computer from going to sleep or activating the
            screensaver when certain applications are running.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://extensions.gnome.org/extension/3193/blur-my-shell/">Blur my Shell</a
            >: Adds a blur effect to the GNOME shell elements for a sleek
            look.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://extensions.gnome.org/extension/3195/vitals/">Vitals</a
            >: Monitors system resources like CPU, RAM, and network usage
            directly from the top bar.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want even more customization, consider <a
          href="https://kde.org/plasma-desktop">KDE Plasma</a
        > desktop environment. Personally, I prefer GNOME for its simplicity. I've
        used KDE before when I was still on Debian, but one thing I noticed is that
        it takes up more power and resources, and since I use a laptop, I prefer
        to save battery life with GNOME.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://gdm-settings.github.io/">GDM settings</a
        >: lock screen customization, auto login, etc. Install GDM settings
        with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install gdm-settings -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 >Basic Rice</h2>
    <p>
      Simple GNOME desktop rice using <a
        href="https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch">fastfetch</a
      >.
    </p>
    <Figure src="rice.webp" alt="GNOME desktop with a fastfetch system summary, a UCLA Bruins logo and a cava audio visualizer in terminals" width="70%" />
    Display any image on the terminal using <a
      href="https://github.com/hpjansson/chafa">chafa</a
    >:
    <CodeBlock is:raw>
      ucla-logo() {
          # Generate the logo on the fly
          local LOGO=$(chafa --size 50 --symbols block /home/img/ucla.png)

          # Print it
          echo -e "$LOGO"
      }
    </CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/karlstav/cava">cava</a>: Audio visualizer
      that draws bars in the terminal from whatever is playing. Install with:
    </p>
    <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install cava -y</ShellScript>
    <p>
      Run it with <C>cava</C>, and press <C>q</C> to quit. Colors, bar width
      and the number of bars are set in <C>~/.config/cava/config</C>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 >Change Some Default Behaviors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Change keybinding: on Linux, copy and paste in the terminal is
        defaulted as Ctrl+Shift+C and Ctrl+Shift+V. To change it to Ctrl+C and
        Ctrl+V, open the terminal, then go to the burger menu, then
        Preferences > Shortcuts, and change the Copy and Paste shortcuts.
        After changing, restart the terminal. Now, Ctrl+Shift+C will be
        automatically mapped to cancel command (SIGINT), and Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V
        will work as expected for copy and paste

        <section>
          <h3>A quick word about Emacs</h3>

          Please note that if you change this keybinding, and you're using
          Emacs, it will interfere with Emacs keybindings in the terminal. For
          example, Ctrl+C is used for various commands in Emacs. If you want
          to use Emacs keybindings, you can either keep the default
          Ctrl+Shift+C and Ctrl+Shift+V for copy and paste, or you can remap
          the copy and paste shortcuts to something else that doesn't conflict
          with Emacs.
        </section>
      </li>
      <li>
        Better Bash autocompletion: Install bash-completion for improved
        command-line experience:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
          sudo apt install bash-completion -y
        </ShellScript>
        After installation, verify, and then source it:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          echo $BASH_VERSION
          source /etc/bash_completion
        </ShellScript>
        Next, add the following line to your <C>~/.bashrc</C> file to enable bash-completion automatically on terminal startup:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          if [ -f /etc/bash_completion ]; then
              . /etc/bash_completion
          fi
        </CodeBlock>
        The next thing I did is to enable a menu-style tab completion by adding
        the following lines to <C>~/.inputrc</C>:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          # Show all matches immediately
          set show-all-if-ambiguous on

          # Enable menu-style completion
          TAB: menu-complete

          # Case-insensitive completion
          set completion-ignore-case on
          set completion-map-case on

          # Mark symlinked directories
          set mark-symlinked-directories on
        </CodeBlock>
        Finally, reload it with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>bind -f ~/.inputrc</ShellScript>
        How this works: let's say you're in a folder called UCLA, with the following
        folders inside:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          ls UCLA
          CS-111  CS-180  CS-35L  CS-M51A  ECE-102 ECE-115C
        </ShellScript>
        now, when you press CS + Tab, it will rotate through different options of
        the CS folders. Then simply press enter to select the desired folder.
      </li>
      <li>
        Remap certain keys (optional). One of the annoying things about
        Laptops made in recent years is that they come with the copilot key,
        which is located where the right Ctrl key should be. To remap it back
        to Ctrl, first install keyd to tell which keycode corresponds to the
        copilot key. keyd isn't in Ubuntu 24.04's repositories, so add its PPA
        first:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo add-apt-repository ppa:keyd-team/ppa
          sudo apt update && sudo apt install keyd -y
          sudo systemctl enable keyd
          sudo systemctl start keyd
        </ShellScript>
        After installation, remember to verify with
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          whereis keyd
          systemctl status keyd
        </ShellScript>

        Next, monitor which keycode corresponds to the copilot key by running:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo keyd monitor # or sudo keyd.rvaiya monitor</ShellScript>
        Add the following to <C>/etc/keyd/default.conf</C>:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          [ids]
          *

          [main]
          leftshift+leftmeta+f23 = rightcontrol # this varies, adjust accordingly to your system
        </ShellScript>
        Then, reload with
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo keyd reload # or sudo keyd.rvaiya reload</ShellScript>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 >DevOps and Text Editors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/">VSCode</a>: My
        primary code editor. Simply use the Software Center to install it via
        snap. If you dislike snap, download the <C>.deb</C> from the <a
          href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download">VSCode website</a
        > and install it; this also adds Microsoft's apt repository, so
        VSCode updates with the rest of the system:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          cd ~/Downloads
          sudo apt install ./code_*.deb
        </ShellScript>

        <strong>Settings:</strong> on Linux, again, copy and paste is defaulted
        as Ctrl+Shift+C and Ctrl+Shift+V. To change it back to Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V,
        go to Settings > Keyboard Shortcuts, and paste the following JSON:
        <CodeBlock language="json" is:raw>
          [
            {
                "key": "ctrl+c",
                "command": "workbench.action.terminal.copySelection",
                "when": "terminalFocus && terminalProcessSupported && terminalTextSelected"
            },
            {
                "key": "ctrl+v",
                "command": "workbench.action.terminal.paste",
                "when": "terminalFocus && terminalProcessSupported"
            },
            {
                "key": "ctrl+shift+c",
                "command": "workbench.action.terminal.sendSequence",
                "args": { "text": "\u0003" },
                "when": "terminalFocus"
            }
          ]
        </CodeBlock>

        <section>
          <h3>A quick note about Snap</h3>

          Snap is one of the most common criticisms of Ubuntu, mostly because
          of perceived performance issues. I am not a big fan of it either.
          That said, it is worth remembering that you don't have to use Snap
          at all if it does not fit your needs. Like many things in Linux, it
          comes down to preference and the flexibility to choose what works
          best for you.
        </section>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://neovim.io/">Neovim</a>: Terminal-based
        text editor. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
          sudo apt install neovim -y
        </ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://git-scm.com/">Git</a>: Version control
        system. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
          sudo apt install git -y
        </ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.docker.com/">Docker</a>:
        Containerization platform. Follow the <a
          href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/">official installation guide</a
        > (install from Docker's apt repository rather than Ubuntu's older
        <C>docker.io</C> package). Then verify the installation:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          sudo systemctl status docker
          sudo docker run hello-world
        </ShellScript>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Programming</h2>
    <section>
      <h3>C, C++, and the GNU Toolchain</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Essentials: Core development tools including build-essential, gdb,
          g++-multilib, and related packages. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install build-essential lldb ninja-build clang gdb g++-multilib -y</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://cmake.org/">CMake</a> and CMake GUI: Build
          system generator with GUI support for easier project configuration. Install
          with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install cmake cmake-qt-gui -y</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangFormat.html">clang-format</a> and
          <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/extra/clang-tidy/">clang-tidy</a>: Code
          formatter and linter. Both pick up a <C>.clang-format</C> /
          <C>.clang-tidy</C> file in the project root, and VSCode's C/C++
          extension uses them automatically. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install clang-format clang-tidy -y</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.nasm.us/">NASM</a>: x86 assembler. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install nasm -y</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          Package Managers:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg">Vcpkg</a
              >: Microsoft's C++ package manager. Install with:
              <ShellScript is:raw>
                cd ~
                git clone https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg
                ./vcpkg/bootstrap-vcpkg.sh
                export VCPKG_ROOT=~/vcpkg
                export PATH=$VCPKG_ROOT:$PATH
              </ShellScript>
              Add the export lines to your .bashrc file to make them permanent.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://conan.io/">Conan</a>: Alternative C++
              package manager. Ubuntu blocks <C>pip install</C> into the system
              Python, so install it as a standalone tool with pipx:
              <ShellScript is:raw>
                sudo apt install pipx -y
                pipx ensurepath
                pipx install conan
              </ShellScript>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          GPU Programming:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-toolkit"
                >CUDA Toolkit</a
              >: GPU programming framework. Follow the installation instructions
              on the
              <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-downloads"
                >NVIDIA website</a
              >. After installation, add these lines to your .bashrc:
              <CodeBlock is:raw>
                # CUDA Toolkit paths
                export PATH=/usr/local/cuda-13.1/bin:$PATH
                export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/cuda-13.1/lib64:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
              </CodeBlock>
              Replace "13.1" with your installed version. Update the environment:
              <ShellScript is:raw>source ~/.bashrc</ShellScript>
              to update the environment variables. Verify the installation by running:
              <ShellScript is:raw>nvcc --version</ShellScript>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cudnn"
                  >cuDNN</a
                >: Deep learning library. Follow the instructions on the <a
                  href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cudnn">NVIDIA cuDNN website</a
                >.
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/nsight-compute">Nsight Compute</a
                >: Kernel-level profiler for CUDA. Download the Linux installer from
                NVIDIA and run it; it installs to
                <C>/usr/local/NVIDIA-Nsight-Compute</C>. Profile from the
                terminal with <C>ncu ./my_program</C>, or open the GUI with
                <C>ncu-ui</C>.
              </li>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <li>
            Profiling:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://valgrind.org/">Valgrind</a>: Memory
                debugging, leak detection, and profiling tool. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install valgrind -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                Perf: Performance analysis tool. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install linux-tools-common linux-tools-$(uname -r) -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            Common Libraries:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.boost.org/">Boost</a>:
                Comprehensive C++ library collection. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libboost-all-dev -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/">Eigen</a>:
                C++ template library for linear algebra. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libeigen3-dev -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.open-mpi.org/">OpenMPI</a>:
                Parallel programming framework. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libopenmpi-dev openmpi-bin -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://fmt.dev/">fmt</a>: Fast, type-safe string
                formatting (the library <C>std::format</C> is based on). Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libfmt-dev -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.sfml-dev.org/">SFML</a>: Simple 2D graphics,
                window, audio and input library, good for small games and
                visualizations. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libsfml-dev -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
              <li>
                OpenGL: <a href="https://www.glfw.org/">GLFW</a> for windows and
                input, <a href="https://github.com/g-truc/glm">GLM</a> for vector
                and matrix math, and the Mesa OpenGL headers. Install with:
                <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install libglfw3-dev libglm-dev libgl1-mesa-dev -y</ShellScript>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Python and Anaconda</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.python.org/">Python</a>: Most
          Linux distributions include Python by default. Check your version:
          <ShellScript is:raw>python3 --version</ShellScript>
          To prevent pip from installing packages globally (which helps keep your
          system clean), create a pip configuration file:
          <ShellScript is:raw>
            mkdir -p ~/.config/pip
            echo "[global]" > ~/.config/pip/pip.conf
            echo "user = true" >> ~/.config/pip/pip.conf
          </ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.anaconda.com/">Anaconda</a>:
          Python development and package management platform. Download the
          installer from the
          <a
            href="https://www.anaconda.com/docs/getting-started/anaconda/install#macos-linux-installation:how-do-i-verify-my-installers-integrity">Anaconda website</a
          > and follow the installation instructions. Verify installation:
          <ShellScript is:raw>
            source ~/.bashrc
            conda --version
          </ShellScript>
          Managing your Anaconda environments:
          <ul>
            <li>
              Prevent Auto-Activation: If you don't want the (base)
              environment to start every time you open a terminal, run:
              <ShellScript is:raw>conda config --set auto_activate false</ShellScript>
            </li>
            <li>
              Create a New Environment: To create a new environment with a
              specific Python version, run:
              <ShellScript is:raw>conda create -n myenv python=3.10</ShellScript>
            </li>
            <li>
              Activate an Environment: To activate an environment, run:
              <ShellScript is:raw>conda activate myenv</ShellScript>
            </li>
            <li>
              Deactivate an Environment: To deactivate the current
              environment, run:
              <ShellScript is:raw>conda deactivate</ShellScript>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <strong>Note:</strong> The global pip restriction mentioned earlier
          can interfere with pip usage inside Anaconda environments. To temporarily
          override it, prefix your pip commands with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>PIP_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV=false</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://jupyter.org/">Jupyter</a>: Notebooks for interactive
          Python. Anaconda's base environment already includes JupyterLab; in
          another conda environment, install it with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>conda install jupyterlab</ShellScript>
          Then start it from your project folder with <C>jupyter lab</C>. VSCode
          can also open <C>.ipynb</C> files directly with the Jupyter extension.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Rust and Cargo</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.rust-lang.org/">Rust</a> and Cargo:
          Systems programming language with built-in package manager. Install
          with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>
            curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh
            source $HOME/.cargo/env
          </ShellScript>
        </li>
        Verify the installation by running:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          rustc --version
          cargo --version
        </ShellScript>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>JavaScript and Web</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://nodejs.org/">Node.js</a> and npm: JavaScript
          runtime and package manager. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install nodejs npm -y</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm">nvm</a>: Node version manager.
          Ubuntu's <C>nodejs</C> package lags behind; nvm installs any Node
          version per user and switches between them. Install it with the
          one-line script in the <a
            href="https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm#installing-and-updating">nvm README</a
          > (the URL changes with every release), run <C>source ~/.bashrc</C>,
          then install and use the latest LTS release:
          <ShellScript is:raw>
            nvm install --lts
            nvm use --lts
            node --version
          </ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          Yarn: Alternative JavaScript package manager. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>npm install -g yarn</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          Global npm tools I use:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/GoogleChrome/lighthouse">Lighthouse</a>:
              Audits a web page's performance, accessibility and SEO from the
              terminal, e.g. <C>lighthouse https://example.com --view</C>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://yeoman.io/">Yeoman</a> and
              <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/generator-code">generator-code</a>:
              Scaffold a new VSCode extension with <C>yo code</C>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@expo/ngrok">@expo/ngrok</a>:
              Tunnels a local dev server to a public URL, used by Expo to test
              apps on a phone.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <ShellScript is:raw>npm install -g lighthouse yo generator-code @expo/ngrok</ShellScript>
        </li>
        <li>
          Cwebp: WebP image conversion tool. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install webp -y</ShellScript>
          Now, you can convert images to WebP format using the cwebp command.
          For example:
          <CodeBlock is:raw>
            # Convert PNG to WebP
            cwebp input.png -o output.webp

            # Batch convert all PNGs
            for file in *.png; do cwebp "$file" -o "${file%.png}.webp"; done
          </CodeBlock>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>C# and .NET</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/">.NET SDK</a>: Compiler, runtime
          and <C>dotnet</C> CLI for C#. Ubuntu 24.04 ships it in its own
          repositories. Install with:
          <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install dotnet-sdk-10.0 -y</ShellScript>
          Verify, then create and run a console app:
          <ShellScript is:raw>
            dotnet --version
            dotnet new console -o hello
            cd hello && dotnet run
          </ShellScript>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 >Engineering Softwares</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html">MATLAB</a
        >: Numerical computing environment. Download from MathWorks and
        follow their installation instructions. To add MATLAB to your
        application menu, create
        <C>~/.local/share/applications/matlab.desktop</C>
        with this content:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          [Desktop Entry]
          Version=1.0
          Type=Application
          Name=MATLAB
          Comment=MATLAB Numerical Computing Environment
          Exec=/usr/local/MATLAB/R2025b/bin/matlab -desktop
          Icon=/usr/local/MATLAB/R2025b/bin/glnxa64/cef_resources/matlab_icon.png
          Terminal=false
          Categories=Development;Science;Engineering;
        </CodeBlock>
        Replace "R2025b" here and below with your installed release.

        Then, refresh the application menu by running:
        <ShellScript is:raw>update-desktop-database ~/.local/share/applications/</ShellScript>

        Note that in some cases, Linux might not let you update or install new packages due to MATLAB installation not having access to root. If that's the case, then you need to give yourself ownership of the MATLAB install:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /usr/local/MATLAB/R2025b</ShellScript>
        then ensure write permission
        <ShellScript is:raw>chmod -R u+rw /usr/local/MATLAB/R2025b</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.kicad.org/">KiCAD</a>: PCB design
        software. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install kicad -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.analog.com/en/resources/design-tools-and-calculators/ltspice-simulator.html">LTspice</a
        >: Circuit simulation tool. LTSpice is mainly a Windows
        application, but it can be run on Linux using Wine. Follow
        instruction on how to install wine on Ubuntu on their <a
          href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/wikis/Debian-Ubuntu">official website</a
        >. Next, download and install LTspice (64 bit) using Wine:
        <ShellScript is:raw>
          cd ~/Downloads
          wget https://LTspice.analog.com/download/latest/LTspice64.msi
          wine msiexec /i LTspice64.msi
        </ShellScript>
        Next, simply start and run the application with
        <ShellScript is:raw>wine ~/.wine/drive_c/users/$USER/AppData/Local/Programs/ADI/LTspice/LTspice.exe</ShellScript>
        You can also create a desktop shortcut for easier access. First, create
        the launcher file. Run this command to create a new .desktop file:
        <ShellScript is:raw>nano ~/.local/share/applications/ltspice.desktop</ShellScript>
        Then, add the following content to the file:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          [Desktop Entry]
          Name=LTspice
          Comment=Analog Devices LTspice Circuit Simulator
          Exec=wine /home/don-le/.wine/drive_c/users/don-le/AppData/Local/Programs/ADI/LTspice/LTspice.exe
          Icon=application-x-ms-dos-executable
          Terminal=false
          Type=Application
          Categories=Education;Electronics;Engineering;
        </CodeBlock>
        Finally, make it executable
        <ShellScript is:raw>chmod +x ~/.local/share/applications/ltspice.desktop</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.amd.com/en/products/software/adaptive-socs-and-fpgas/vivado.html">AMD Vivado</a
        >: FPGA design suite (synthesis, implementation, simulation) for AMD/Xilinx
        boards. Download the Linux self-extracting web installer from the <a
          href="https://www.xilinx.com/support/download.html">AMD download page</a
        > (requires a free AMD account) and follow the installation guide for
        your release; the free Standard edition covers most hobby and student
        boards. Check that your Ubuntu version is on the release's supported
        list, and install the cable drivers too if you want to program a board
        over USB.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.audacityteam.org/">Audacity</a>:
        Audio editing software. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install audacity -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://tug.org/texlive/">TeX Live</a> and
        <a href="https://ctan.org/pkg/latexmk">latexmk</a>: LaTeX distribution
        and build tool. Rather than the multi-gigabyte <C>texlive-full</C>, I
        install the collections I actually use (math, science, XeTeX, journal
        classes):
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install texlive-latex-extra texlive-science texlive-xetex texlive-fonts-recommended texlive-publishers texlive-plain-generic texlive-extra-utils latexmk -y</ShellScript>
        Then build a document, rerunning BibTeX and cross-references as
        needed, with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>latexmk -pdf main.tex</ShellScript>
        Use <C>latexmk -xelatex</C> for documents that need system fonts, and
        <C>latexmk -pvc -pdf main.tex</C> to rebuild on every save.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://pandoc.org/">Pandoc</a>: Converts documents between
        formats: Markdown, LaTeX, HTML, Word and more. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install pandoc -y</ShellScript>
        For example:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          # Markdown to PDF (uses TeX Live)
          pandoc notes.md -o notes.pdf

          # Word to Markdown
          pandoc report.docx -o report.md
        </CodeBlock>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Media and Graphics</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://ffmpeg.org/">FFmpeg</a>: Converts, trims and compresses
        audio and video from the terminal. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install ffmpeg -y</ShellScript>
        For example:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          # Convert a video to MP4 (H.264)
          ffmpeg -i input.mov -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -c:a aac output.mp4

          # Extract the audio track
          ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vn -c:a libmp3lame audio.mp3
        </CodeBlock>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.videolan.org/vlc/"
          >VLC Media Player</a
        >: Versatile media player. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install vlc</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://imagemagick.org/">ImageMagick</a>: Command-line image
        editing: resize, crop, convert between formats. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install imagemagick -y</ShellScript>
        For example:
        <CodeBlock is:raw>
          # Resize to 50%
          convert input.jpg -resize 50% output.jpg

          # Resize every JPG in place to at most 1600px wide
          mogrify -resize '1600x>' *.jpg
        </CodeBlock>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.darktable.org/">darktable</a>: RAW photo editor,
        an open-source alternative to Lightroom. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install darktable -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://shotwell-project.org/">Shotwell</a>: Photo manager for
        organizing and importing photos from a camera. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install shotwell -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://kdenlive.org/">Kdenlive</a>: Video editor. The snap
        is more up to date than the apt package. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo snap install kdenlive</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://flameshot.org/">Flameshot</a>: Screenshot tool
        with in-place annotation (arrows, boxes, blur, text) before you save
        or copy. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install flameshot -y</ShellScript>
        To bind it to a key, open Settings &gt; Keyboard &gt; View and Customize
        Shortcuts &gt; Custom Shortcuts, add a shortcut with the command
        <C>flameshot gui</C>, and assign it a key such as Print Screen
        (remove that key from the built-in screenshot shortcut first).
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://wiki.gnome.org/Apps/Cheese">Cheese</a>: Webcam app for
        photos and videos, handy for checking that the camera works. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo apt install cheese -y</ShellScript>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Other Softwares</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.mozilla.org/firefox/">Firefox</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.spotify.com/">Spotify</a>: Install the snap from the
        Software Center, or add Spotify's apt repository with the commands on
        their <a href="https://www.spotify.com/download/linux/">Linux download page</a
        >. Spotify replaces its signing key every year, so copy the commands
        from that page rather than from an old guide.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://code-industry.net/masterpdfeditor/"
          >Master PDF Editor</a
        >: PDF editing software. Installation steps:
        <ul>
          <li>
            First, download the installer from the Master PDF Editor
            website.
          </li>
          <li>
            Next, open the terminal and navigate to the directory where
            the installer was downloaded, then install it via apt.
            <ShellScript is:raw>
              cd ~/Downloads
              sudo apt install ./master-pdf-editor*.deb
            </ShellScript>
          </li>
          <li>
            Then, run the installer with: <C>./master-pdf-editor-*.run</C>
          </li>
          <li>
            Follow the on-screen instructions to complete the
            installation.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://telegram.org/">Telegram</a>:
        Messaging app. Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo snap install telegram-desktop</ShellScript>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/">Steam</a>: Game store and
        launcher. Windows-only games run through Proton, which Steam enables by
        default for verified titles (Settings &gt; Compatibility to enable it
        for all games). Install with:
        <ShellScript is:raw>sudo snap install steam</ShellScript>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

